*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   SCREEN RUN f {RETURNING id} {THEN ... ELSE ...}
*
*   is compiled into
*
*   call !screen_run(f, ret, status)
*
*     f        the form (SCREEN FORM and the verbs that add widgets)
*     ret      the id of the button that ended the form; for Esc, the id of the form's cancel button,
*              or null if it has none
*     status   0 a button was pressed; 1 Esc or the cancel button was, or the input ended, or f is not a
*              form (ELSE)
*
*   The form is drawn over the screen (what is under it is kept, and put back when it ends), the focus
*   starts on the first widget that can have it, or where it was left by a run before, and the keys are
*   those of $sl_form: Tab and Shift-Tab, the arrows, Return (the default button), Esc (cancel), and
*   what each widget takes.  What the user typed stays in the widgets: read it with SCREEN GET, or run the
*   form again to let the user go on.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine screen_run(f, ret, status)
$catalog !screen_run

$include sl.h

   ret = ''
   status = 1
   if system(25) then return                  ;* a phantom has no terminal: nothing is drawn, nobody is waited for
   if jtype(f) # 'collection' then return
   call $sl_scr('get', scr)

   call $sl_form('run', f, scr, '', r)
   call $sl_scr('put', scr)

   ret = r<2>
   if r<1> = 'done' then status = 0

   return
end

* END-CODE
